  "text": "Question eleven. What is windowing, and what are the main window types? Windowing cuts an endless stream into finite buckets you can aggregate. Tumbling windows are fixed and never overlap. Ten seconds each, so every click lands in exactly one: five, four, three. Sliding windows, also called hopping, are fixed but overlap. Ten seconds long, starting every five, so each click counts in two. Session windows have no fixed size: one closes after a gap of inactivity. With a five-second gap, ava's five clicks make two sessions, which is how you sessionize users.\n\nQuestion twelve. What is backpressure, and how do streaming systems handle it? Backpressure is a consumer that can't keep up. Kafka absorbs it: the log is durable, so a slow consumer falls behind and nothing is dropped. Push-based systems push back instead, and slow the producer. Watch consumer lag: the distance from the latest offset to the committed one. Model it: about a hundred messages a second arrive, and one consumer handles eighty. After a minute, lag is over eleven hundred. A second consumer, on a partition of its own, clears it in eighteen seconds. Rising lag means more partitions and consumers, or faster processing.",
